-- title:
--   Affinoid meromorphy of pulled-back sections on Čerednik–Drinfeld quotients
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $r$ is a prime number and $\mathcal O$ is a characteristic-zero domain which is a discrete valuation ring, $\pi \in \mathcal O$ is irreducible, $\mathcal O$ is $\pi$-adically complete, the residue ring $\mathcal O/(\pi)$ has exactly $r$ elements, and $(r) = (\pi)$ as ideals of $\mathcal O$; $K_0$ is a characteristic-zero field which is a fraction field of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   **Unramified coefficients.** $Onr$ is a characteristic-zero domain and $\mathcal O$-algebra, $Fr$ an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism of it, subject to: $Onr$ is adically complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$ (`hOnr_complete`), that ideal is maximal (`hOnr_max`), every $x \in Onr$ satisfies a monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial over $Onr$ of positive degree has a root modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_closed`), and $Fr(x) \equiv x^{r} \pmod \pi$ for all $x$ (`hFr`).
--
--   **Determinant valuation.** $vdet : \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0) \to \mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) is a homomorphism such that $vdet(g) = n$ holds exactly when $\det g = u\,\pi^{n}$ for some unit $u$ of $\mathcal O$ (`hvdet`).
--
--   **Group data.** $G$ is a group, $\sigma : G \to \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ a homomorphism, $\Gamma \le G$ a subgroup containing an element $z$ with $\sigma z$ a scalar matrix and $vdet(\sigma z) = 2$ (`hcent`) and an element $w$ with $vdet(\sigma w) = 1$ (`hodd`); $\Gamma'$ is a subgroup whose elements are exactly those $x \in \Gamma$ with $vdet(\sigma x)$ even (`hΓ'`). The homomorphism $\rho : G \to \mathrm{PGL}_2(K_0)$ is the projectivisation of $\sigma$ (`hρ`), and the image $\rho(\Gamma')$ acts on the vertices of the lattice tree of $(\mathcal O, K_0)$ with finite vertex stabilisers (`hdisc`) and with finitely many orbits, witnessed by a finite set of vertices meeting every orbit (`hcocpt`).
--
--   **The quotient datum.** $\mathcal X$ is a scheme and $f : \mathcal X \to \operatorname{Spec} \mathcal O$ is proper and flat. For every $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which the image of $\pi$ is nilpotent, $\Theta$ assigns to each pair consisting of an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $Onr \to B$ and a Deligne datum over $B$ (a family of $B$-submodules $\mathrm{line}(M) \subseteq B \otimes_{\mathcal O} M$, indexed by the full $\mathcal O$-lattices $M \subseteq K_0^2$, with invertible quotients, monotone under inclusions of lattices, equivariant for homothety, and nondegenerate at every prime of $B$) a point of $\mathcal X$ over $B$, that is, a morphism $\operatorname{Spec} B \to \mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$. Four conditions are imposed: $\Theta$ is natural in $B$ along $\mathcal O$-algebra maps (`hΘnat`); $\Theta$ is invariant under the twisted $\Gamma$-action, in the sense that if $\gamma \in \Gamma$ and $x, x'$ satisfy `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct` for $\sigma\gamma$ — the first components are related by precomposition with $Fr^{-vdet(\sigma\gamma)}$ and the second by pullback of the Deligne datum along $(\sigma\gamma)^{-1}$ — then $\Theta(x') = \Theta(x)$ (`hΘinv`); for an algebraically closed field $k$ which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra with $\pi$ nilpotent and a fixed $\psi : Onr \to k$, the map $P \mapsto \Theta(\psi, P)$ is surjective onto the $k$-points of $f$ and its fibres are exactly the twisted $\Gamma$-orbits (`hΘfib`); and $\Theta$ is initial among such data: for every $t : T \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and every family $\rho'$ of the same shape which is natural and twisted-$\Gamma$-invariant there is a natural family $u$ of maps from the points of $f$ to the points of $t$ with $u \circ \Theta = \rho'$, and any natural $u'$ with $u' \circ \Theta = \rho'$ coincides with $u$ (`hΘuniv`).
--
--   **Analytic frame.** $C$ is an algebraically closed $K_0$-algebra field, complete for a valuation with values in a linearly ordered commutative group with zero $\Gamma_0$; $R$ is a commutative ring, an $\mathcal O$-algebra, with $C$ an $R$-algebra and the evident scalar towers. $\varpi$ is a pseudo-uniformiser: an element $\varpi.\varpi \in K_0$ whose image in $C$ has valuation strictly between $0$ and $1$ and such that the valuation of every nonzero element of $K_0$ lies between $|\varpi|^{N}$ and $|\varpi|^{-N}$ for some $N$. The hypothesis `hF` asserts that $(\pi, \varpi, R)$ is an adic frame: $\pi$ is irreducible, $R \to C$ is injective with image exactly the valuation ring $\{v \le 1\}$ of $C$, $R$ is $\pi$-adically complete, the elements of $K_0$ of valuation $\le 1$ are exactly those coming from $\mathcal O$, the image of $K_0$ in $C$ is closed, and the image of $\pi$ in $C$ equals that of $\varpi.\varpi$. Further, $\psi_0 : Onr \to R$ is an $\mathcal O$-algebra map; `hrk` states that for $x, y \in C$ with $v(x) < 1$ and $y \ne 0$ there is $n$ with $v(x)^n \le v(y)$; `hval` states that every nonzero $\varepsilon \in \Gamma_0$ dominates the valuation of some nonzero element of $C$; `hex` states that the standard affinoids $\Omega_n = \{z \in C : v(z) \le |\varpi|^{-n} \text{ and } |\varpi|^{n} \le v(z - a) \text{ for all } a \in K_0 \text{ with } v(a) \le |\varpi|^{-n}\}$ exhaust the Drinfeld upper half-plane $\Omega_C = C \setminus K_0$; and the ring `Omega.HolRingOf` $\varpi\,\rho$ of functions on $\Omega_C$ holomorphic on every affinoid is assumed to be a domain.
--
--   **Uniformisation.** $\Phi$ sends each adic point of the formal upper half-plane over $R$ — a compatible system, for $n \in \mathbb N$, of Deligne data over $R/(\pi^{n+1})$ — to an $R$-point of $\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec} R$. The hypothesis `hΦ` has four parts: the reduction of $\Phi(x)$ modulo $\pi^{n+1}$ is $\Theta$ applied to the reduction of $\psi_0$ and to the $n$-th component of $x$; $\Phi$ is surjective; $\Phi(x) = \Phi(x')$ holds precisely when $x' = x \cdot \sigma\gamma$ for some $\gamma \in \Gamma'$; and base change along $R \to C$ is injective on such $R$-points and hits every $C$-point of $\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$.
--
--   **Base change and the three geometric hypotheses.** $s_C : \operatorname{Spec} C \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ is the morphism induced by $\mathcal O \to K_0 \to C$ (`hsC`), and the pullback $\mathcal X_C = \mathcal X \times_{\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O} \operatorname{Spec} C$ is assumed integral. The hypothesis `hcover` (uniform algebraic clearing over a finite cover) requires: for every open $U \subseteq \mathcal X_C$ containing the generic point and every $s \in \Gamma(U, \mathcal X_C)$ there are finitely many opens $V_a \subseteq \mathcal X$ ($a \in \mathrm{Fin}\,\ell$) such that every adic point $x$ has $\Phi(x)$ factoring through some $V_a$, and for each $a$ there are an open $W \subseteq \mathcal X_C$ contained in the preimage of $V_a$ under the first projection and containing the generic point, finite families $num_i, den_j \in \Gamma(V_a, \mathcal X)$ and scalars $cnum_i, cden_j \in C$ (the scalars read as global sections of $\mathcal X_C$ through the second projection) such that the section $D = \sum_j cden_j \cdot den_j|_W$ of $\Gamma(W, \mathcal X_C)$ is nonzero and $s \cdot D = \big(\sum_i cnum_i \cdot num_i|_W\big)$ after restriction to $W \cap U$. The hypothesis `htube` (tubewise holomorphy of pulled-back sections) requires: for every $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$, every open $V \subseteq \mathcal X$ and every $p \in \Gamma(V, \mathcal X)$, there are finitely many pairs of finite sets $L_k, M_k \subseteq C \times C$, all second coordinates nonzero, such that, with $E(g)$ the union of the vertex tube of $\bar g$, the edge tube of $\bar g$ and the vertex tube of $\overline{g \cdot \mathrm{diag}(\varpi,1)}$ (tubes taken with respect to the Möbius action `Omega.pmoebius` of $\mathrm{PGL}_2(K_0)$ on $C$) and with $A_k \subseteq E(g)$ cut out by the inequalities $v(\varrho) \le v(\bar g^{-1}z - e)$ for $(e,\varrho) \in L_k$ and $v(\bar g^{-1}z - e) \le v(\varrho)$ for $(e,\varrho) \in M_k$: every adic point $x$ whose coordinate $z = x.\mathrm{toOmega}\,C$ lies in $E(g)$ and whose $\Phi(x)$ factors through $V$ has $z \in A_k$ for some $k$; conversely, if the coordinate of an adic point lies in some $A_k$ then $\Phi(x)$ factors through $V$; and for each $k$ there is $F_k : A_k \to C$ lying in `Omega.holOn` $C\,A_k$ (a uniform limit of uniformly bounded rational functions without poles on $A_k$) whose value at the coordinate of any adic point $x$ with $\Phi(x)$ factoring through $V$ is the image in $C$ of the global value over $\operatorname{Spec} R$ of the pullback of $p$ along $\Phi(x)$. The hypothesis `hfin` (affinoid finiteness of exceptional coordinates) requires that for every open $U \subseteq \mathcal X_C$ containing the generic point and every $n$ the set of $z \in \Omega_n$ which are coordinates of adic points $x$ admitting a $C$-point $q$ of $\mathcal X_C$ with first projection the base change of $\Phi(x)$, second projection the identity of $\operatorname{Spec} C$, and $q$ not factoring through $U$, is finite.
--
--   **Conclusion.** Under these hypotheses, let $U \subseteq \mathcal X_C$ be an open containing the generic point, $s \in \Gamma(U, \mathcal X_C)$, and $F : \Omega_C \to C$ a function which represents $s$ in the following sense: whenever $z \in \Omega_C$ is the coordinate $x.\mathrm{toOmega}\,C$ of an adic point $x$ and $q : \operatorname{Spec} C \to \mathcal X_C$ has first projection the base change of $\Phi(x)$, second projection the identity, and factors through $U$, then $F(z)$ equals the value of $s$ at $q$ (the global section $q^{*}s$ over $\operatorname{Spec} C$, read as an element of $C$). Then for every $n \in \mathbb N$ there exist functions $a, b : \Omega_n \to C$ such that $a$ and $b$ both lie in `Omega.holOn` $C\,\Omega_n$, $b$ is not identically zero on $\Omega_n$, and for every $z \in \Omega_n$ with $b(z) \ne 0$ one has $b(z)\,F(z) = a(z)$, where $F$ is evaluated at $z$ viewed in $\Omega_C$ through the inclusion $\Omega_n \subseteq \Omega_C$. No relation between the pairs $(a,b)$ for different $n$ is asserted, and the identity is asserted only off the zero locus of $b$.
--
--   This is the affinoid-local step of the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation argument: the pullback to the Drinfeld upper half-plane of a rational section of the integral curve $\mathcal X_C$ is shown to be meromorphic on each standard affinoid $\Omega_n$, i.e. a quotient of two rigid-holomorphic functions there. It is used by [`CerednikDrinfeld.exists_invariant_chartwiseMeromorphic_pullback_of_cover_clearing_of_cerednikDrinfeld_quotient`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.exists_invariant_chartwiseMeromorphic_pullback_of_cover_clearing_of_cerednikDrinfeld_quotient), which assembles the affinoid presentations into a $\Gamma'$-invariant chartwise meromorphic function on the whole upper half-plane.
